# 2026-07-30, X-16(b): main.zig +6, RAISED DELIBERATELY. Same change in all four daemons.
#
#   Every fatal startup failure used to be invisible. `daemonize` points stderr at
#   /dev/null, so the error Zig's start code prints for an error returned from `main`
#   goes to a descriptor nobody reads, and syslog -- the only channel still open after
#   the fork -- never received it. Measured: a daemon told to bind an unusable address
#   exited 0, left nothing listening, and logged "starting" as its last word.
#
#   The fix is the SHAPE of `main`, not a line inside it. The body moved to `runDaemon`
#   and `main` became one line:
#
#       runDaemon() catch |e| return bootstrap_mod.fatal(e);
#
#   so every error converges on one report -- including from a `try` added later.
#   Per-site catch blocks were the alternative, and are how X-7 came to be fixed in one
#   of four copies.
#
#   +6 is the entire cost: three lines of `main`, one blank, and a two-line comment
#   pointing at `bootstrap.fatal` where the reasoning is written once. It was +10 until
#   the gate objected to the same six-line rationale appearing in four files, which was
#   a fair objection and is why it now lives in the library.
